WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — spellcheck (90s) Narrator Misha

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.402
First, people once paid hundreds of dollars for standalone spell check,

2
00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:07.907
yet today it comes free everywhere.

3
00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:11.956
Second, it became free because checking a word costs

4
00:00:11.956 --> 00:00:15.437
almost nothing to run inside tools you already use.

5
00:00:15.937 --> 00:00:20.585
Third, new computer intelligence reaches seven billion people only

6
00:00:20.585 --> 00:00:24.177
when it runs cheaply on devices people already own.

7
00:00:24.677 --> 00:00:30.000
Imagine mailing every sentence you write to a distant typing bureau and paying by

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.995
the word just to spot a typo. That sounds ridiculous for spelling today, yet

9
00:00:34.995 --> 00:00:40.384
renting every answer from a remote cloud server is still how frontier AI operates.

10
00:00:40.884 --> 00:00:48.348
In 1982, engineers compressed thirty thousand English words into twenty-six thousand sixteen-bit machine words

11
00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:54.862
so ordinary minicomputers could check documents locally without a network. Within a decade, word

12
00:00:54.862 --> 00:01:01.987
processors, web browsers, and mobile keyboards absorbed spell checking completely at zero separate price.

13
00:01:02.487 --> 00:01:11.692
Our dated price ledger tracks electronic calculators from four-thousand-nine-hundred-dollar desktop units and standalone two-hundred-dollar spell checkers

14
00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:19.642
in 1981 down through eighty-nine-dollar grammar packages to open libraries and compact one-point-four-megabyte phone keyboard models.

15
00:01:20.142 --> 00:01:26.802
Global reach follows the everyday hardware people already carry in their hands—nearly six billion mobile

16
00:01:26.802 --> 00:01:33.974
subscribers and over three hundred billion embedded chips—once verified skills run locally at negligible energy.
